  "prompt": "A medium-sized company wants a generative AI assistant that answers employee questions\nabout policies, retrieves company documents, creates support tickets, drafts emails,\nenforces employee access permissions, requires approval before external emails, and offers\nmeasurable evidence of response reliability.\n\nExplain how you would design the system. Your answer should cover:\n• The AI architecture and use of RAG.\n• Tools, permissions and the agent's decision process.\n• Security guardrails and human oversight.\n• Handling missing evidence or tool failures.\n• At least three methods or metrics for evaluating performance.\nSuggested answer length: 200–300 words.",
  "answer": "A suitable architecture would combine retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with a\ntool-enabled AI agent, using human approval for consequential actions.\nFirst, company policies should be indexed in a retrieval system. Documents would be\ndivided into chunks, converted into embeddings and stored in a searchable index. When\nemployees ask questions, the system retrieves relevant passages and generates answers\nwith citations. Retrieval must enforce the employee's document access permissions.\nThe agent should have narrowly scoped tools for searching documents, creating support\ntickets and drafting emails. A workflow or agent loop would determine which authorized tool\nto use, inspect the results and prepare a response.\nSecurity controls should include least-privilege permissions, input validation, prompt-injection\ndefenses, audit logging, resource limits and data-retention policies. External emails must\nrequire human approval before sending.\nIf retrieval produces insufficient evidence, the assistant should acknowledge uncertainty\nrather than inventing an answer. Failed tool calls should be handled explicitly, with controlled\nretries or escalation.\nEvaluation should use representative test cases and measure grounded accuracy, citation\ncorrectness, tool execution success, security compliance and operational performance such\n\nas latency, token consumption, cost and failure rates. Exact-match checks can validate\nstructured outputs, rubric-based evaluations can assess written responses and periodic\nhuman spot checks can identify errors missed by automated evaluation."
